26:- "Container runtime", the runtime in use, Podman or Docker, as the [ContainerEnvironment](../container-environment/index.md#container-environment) picks it, named in the row with its version, found by running its command with `--version`, required, since Spec tasks, a Chain prompt's spec steps, and questions run in a container. Passing with either; missing both, its guidance links to Podman's and Docker's installation instructions for the platform, as it gives them.
27:- "Runtime machine", on macOS and Windows only, required, passing while the runtime's machine is running, a Podman machine or Docker Desktop. With none, its guidance offers "Set up Podman", and with one stopped, "Start Podman", each as the [ContainerEnvironment](../container-environment/index.md#container-environment) says, what the command prints shown beneath the row as it runs. It is checked only once Podman is found, and shows "Needs Podman", muted, until then.
28:- "Containers", required, passing once Podman runs a container for the application, as by running `podman info`. Failing, its guidance says Podman couldn't be accessed, with what Podman printed, as the [ContainerEnvironment](../container-environment/index.md#container-environment) says. It is checked only once the checks above it pass.
36:"Check again" runs every check anew. The harness checks run anew by themselves whenever another project is opened or the open project's harness is changed, so they never speak of a harness the project doesn't use. A check whose action, as setting up Podman or logging in, finishes runs again by itself, and so do the checks waiting on it. A link opened in the browser leaves the page as it is, until Check again.
44:The welcome page closes as any inset panel does, as the [InsetPanel](../../ui/components/inset-panel/index.md#inset-panel) says: with its close button, its Get started button, <Escape>, or a click on the dimmed window around it, and always, whatever its checks say, failing, passing, or still running, and however it was opened. Closing it never waits on a check, nor on a fix still running, as setting up Podman, which carries on in the background, its check running again once it finishes withou
        out.push_str(rest);
        out
    }

    /// The arguments to `podman` that run `command` with `args` in a
    /// container of `image` as this plan says, its input open; with `tty`,
    /// under a terminal of its own.
    pub fn run_args(
        &self,
        image: &str,
        command: &str,
        args: &[OsString],
        tty: bool,
    ) -> Vec<OsString> {
        let mut out: Vec<OsString> = vec!["run".into(), "--rm".into(), "-i".into()];
        if tty {
            out.push("-t".into());
        }
        // Files it writes belong to the user, as on the host: it runs as the
        // user, never as root, on Linux and inside the Podman machine on
        // macOS and Windows alike, so what it writes into a folder mounted
        // from the host is the user's.
        out.push("--userns=keep-id".into());
        out.extend(["--label".into(), "suspense=run".into()]);
        // Ended first should the machine run out of memory, never Suspense.
        out.push("--oom-score-adj=1000".into());
        out.extend(["-e".into(), format!("HOME={HOME}").into()]);
        // What the harness is given on the host too, as the claude.ai
        // account's connectors turned off.
        for (name, value) in self.agent.env() {
            out.extend(["-e".into(), format!("{name}={value}").into()]);
        }
        // The harness's login and settings, shared by every project.
        out.extend([
            "-v".into(),
            format!("{}:{HOME}:U", home_volume(self.agent)).into(),
        ]);
        // Its conversation's sessions, from the project, where the harness
        // keeps them; no other conversation's.
        if let Some(sessions) = &self.sessions {
            let mut spec: OsString = "type=bind,src=".into();
            spec.push(sessions.as_os_str());
            spec.push(format!(",dst={HOME}/{}", session_dir(self.agent)));
            out.extend(["--mount".into(), spec]);
        }
        for mount in &self.mounts {
            let mut spec: OsString = "type=bind,src=".into();
            spec.push(mount.host.as_os_str());
            spec.push(",dst=");
            spec.push(self.container_path(&mount.host).as_os_str());
            if !mount.writable {
                spec.push(",ro=true");
            }
            out.extend(["--mount".into(), spec]);
        }
        for hidden in &self.hidden {
            let mut spec: OsString = "type=tmpfs,dst=".into();
            spec.push(self.container_path(hidden).as_os_str());
            spec.push(",ro=true");
            out.extend(["--mount".into(), spec]);
        }
        // A guidance file in the code, read only, covered by a scratch file
        // a build can write.
        for (file, scratch) in &self.covered {
            let Some(scratch) = scratch else {
                continue;
            };
            let mut spec: OsString = "type=bind,src=".into();
            spec.push(scratch.as_os_str());
            spec.push(",dst=");
            spec.push(self.container_path(file).as_os_str());
            out.extend(["--mount".into(), spec]);
        }
